<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aina Mahal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aina Mahal ('hall of mirrors') is an 18th-century palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuj">Bhuj</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built for Rao <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakhpatji">Lakhpatji</a> of Cutch. It was designed by Ramsinh Malam, a local craftsman who had learned European crafts during years abroad, and is known for its mirrored hall, Venetian glass and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> tiles. It was badly damaged in the Gujarat earthquake of 2001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aina-mahal.jpg" alt="Aina Mahal" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nizil Shah · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Damaged_Ainamahal_Bhuj_Kutch_Gujarat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Aina Mahal stands next to the Prag Mahal in Darbargadh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuj">Bhuj</a>. Rao <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakhpatji">Lakhpatji</a>, who ruled Cutch from 1741 to 1760, had it built around 1750. Its chief architect and designer, Lakhpatji's master craftsman, had lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for 18 years and mastered several European crafts and building skills. The construction cost 80 lakh koris, equal to three years of the state's revenue at the time. In 1830 an Englishwoman, Mrs. Postnas, visited and later wrote of a room lit by large candles where six musical clocks played at once among jelly glasses and old vases.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The two-storey mansion is built of stone, decorated with stone carving and wood fretwork, and whitewashed outside: local in its design, European in its decoration. Its floors are laid with blue delftware tiles, and its marble walls carry mirrors in gilded frames and shelves for glassware and ceramics. The first floor holds the Audience Hall, the Pleasure Hall, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> and the State Apartments; the second, reached by an outside staircase, holds an antechamber, the Darbar Hall and a Marriage Hall built in 1884 for Maharao Khengarji III.</p><p>In the Hira Mahal, the royal bedchamber, stands a bed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakhpatji">Lakhpatji</a> with legs of pure gold, ringed by more than twenty-seven large mirrors. In the Fuvara Mahal, the Pleasure Hall, almost the whole chamber is a pool with fountains around a raised platform, where the ruler held court on a throne cooled by the spray.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The palace became a museum in 1977. Its collection is an early example of europeanerie, the 18th-century taste of Indian nobles for European things: English, French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> clocks, glassware, mechanical toys, celestial globes and prints of William Hogarth's A Rake's Progress. The antechamber shows Mughal farmans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a> addressed mostly to Rao Tamachi, and a 38.7-metre horoscope of Rao Pragmalji II dated 1839. After the 2001 earthquake part of the building was restored to house the Aina Mahal Trust Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aina Mahal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rao Lakhpatji (r. 1741–1761) patronised poets and craftsmen, and his reign produced the <b>Aina Mahal</b> palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/cutch-state">Cutch State</a></li></ul></section>
